.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help
This "help" flag cause the program to print out a description
of its operation and then exit immediately.
.TP
.B \-\-input=\fIinputGridFile\fP
.br
Sets the filename of the input topography grid. This must be readable
as a netCDF grid with "x"/"y" (or "lon"/"lat") dimension variables and a
"z" data variable. If the supplied name does not already end in ".grd",
the suffix ".grd" is appended to it. This option (or the equivalent short
option \fB\-I\fP) must be specified.
.TP
.B \-\-output=\fIoutputOctreeFile\fP
.br
Sets the filename of the output TRN octree file. If the supplied name does
not already end in ".bo", the suffix ".bo" is appended to it. This option
(or the equivalent short option \fB\-O\fP) must be specified.
.TP
.B \-\-bounds=\fIval1/val2/val3/val4\fP, \fB\-R\fP\fIval1/val2/val3/val4\fP
.br
Sets an optional bounding filter on the grid nodes used to build the
octree, given as four slash\-separated numeric values that must satisfy
\fIval1\fP < \fIval2\fP and \fIval3\fP < \fIval4\fP. Grid nodes are excluded whenever
their north coordinate is less than \fIval3\fP or greater than \fIval4\fP, or
whenever their east coordinate is greater than \fIval1\fP or less than
\fIval2\fP. Setting \fIval1\fP or \fIval2\fP to \-1 disables that particular east/west
test. Because \fIval1\fP must be less than \fIval2\fP, supplying two finite
(non\-\-1) values for both normally causes every node to be excluded on the
east/west axis, so in practice at most one of the two east/west values
should be set to something other than \-1. If \fB\-\-bounds\fP/\fB\-R\fP is omitted
altogether, no additional bounding filter is applied beyond the fixed
depth\-range and no\-data checks described above.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
The \fB\-\-verbose\fP option causes \fBmbgrd2octree\fP to print out status and
debug messages; repeating it (e.g. as \fB\-V\fP \fB\-V\fP) redirects this output
to stderr and increases the level of detail reported.

